Any document that is **not in German or English** has to be submitted together with a translation; the German missions in Turkey and the universities accept translations by sworn translators (yeminli tercüman) and reject plain translations. In the study-applicant information sheet of the German missions in Turkey, the Lise Diploması, the ÖSYM result and placement documents and transcripts are listed as "certified copy + certified translation".

Typically translated for a student visa:

- Lise Diploması or university diploma and transcripts (if not already issued in English by the school or university);
- ÖSYM/YKS result document and placement document for school leavers;
- documents for financing from Turkey, for example a parental sponsorship declaration, bank letters in Turkish, or a court decision on custody for minors;
- civil-status documents if they are requested (birth certificate, marriage certificate), unless issued as multilingual international extracts.

Usually **not** translated: the passport, the admission letter and language certificates (already in German or English), blocked-account confirmations from German providers, German health insurance confirmations.

Two practical rules: have the translation made from a **certified copy** or the original and keep the originals to show at the appointment, and check the **current checklist** of the German mission (via iDATA or the mission's website) before you translate anything, because the list differs between the Istanbul, Ankara and İzmir offices and is updated during the year. Universities and uni-assist have their own translation rules for the application; a translation made for the university can normally be reused for the visa.

Anmeldung is the registration of your home address with the local registration office (Bürgeramt, Einwohnermeldeamt or Kundenzentrum). The legal frame is the Bundesmeldegesetz:

- you must register **within two weeks of moving into** your home (§ 17 BMG);
- the person who lets you the home has to confirm your move-in in writing – the **Wohnungsgeberbestätigung** (§ 19 BMG). Landlords, Studentenwerk halls and main tenants of a shared flat issue it; without it you cannot register.

What to bring: passport (with visa), the Wohnungsgeberbestätigung, the registration form (Anmeldeformular, available online in most cities), and, if applicable, your lease. The office enters you in the register and gives you the **Meldebescheinigung** (registration certificate) on the spot. In large cities appointments are scarce; book online as soon as you have the landlord confirmation, and check whether the city offers online registration (several states have introduced it since 2024).

What the registration triggers:

- the tax office sends your **tax ID** (Steuer-ID) by post within a few weeks;
- the **Rundfunkbeitrag** service writes to you (one fee per household; a flatmate may already pay it);
- the **Ausländerbehörde** of your city becomes responsible for your residence permit; apply there before the visa expires;
- banks, employers and the university use the Meldebescheinigung as proof of address.

If you move within Germany, you register the new address (an Ummeldung) within the same two-week period; if you leave Germany, you deregister (Abmeldung). Turkish citizens are also advised to file an address declaration with the Turkish consulate; that is a separate, Turkish obligation and does not replace the German registration.

Source: §§ 17, 19 Bundesmeldegesetz (gesetze-im-internet.de).
